WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.442 --> 00:00:06.000
- We're getting a couple
questions about being a speaker.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.336
So Esteban saying, how do I
start becoming a paid speaker?

00:00:09.336 --> 00:00:13.471
(mumbles) is asking, how
do you evaluate speakers

00:00:13.471 --> 00:00:14.959
who are coming in?

00:00:14.959 --> 00:00:19.049
So, in terms of being a speaker,
you do a lot of speaking,

00:00:19.049 --> 00:00:21.873
obviously, as part of what you do.

00:00:21.873 --> 00:00:24.652
You've hired speakers to come
in and speak at your events.

00:00:24.652 --> 00:00:26.465
What are your thoughts on people

00:00:26.465 --> 00:00:28.819
getting into the paid speaking business

00:00:28.819 --> 00:00:31.669
and their evolution
advice to help them out?

00:00:31.669 --> 00:00:35.836
- Yeah, I think the parasocial
is really incredible here,

00:00:38.704 --> 00:00:41.837
so I think if you want
to become a paid speaker,

00:00:41.837 --> 00:00:43.357
I think it's fantastic, and obviously

00:00:43.357 --> 00:00:45.088
it could be pretty lucrative.

00:00:45.088 --> 00:00:48.707
Um, again, I think, number
one, you need to have,

00:00:48.707 --> 00:00:50.892
in this case, the product is you.

00:00:50.892 --> 00:00:52.744
So you are the product.

00:00:52.744 --> 00:00:53.577
So what's your idea?

00:00:53.577 --> 00:00:54.410
What's your point of view?

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:57.228
What do you want to be famous for?

00:00:57.228 --> 00:00:59.882
And it could be, you know, you've done,

00:00:59.882 --> 00:01:02.229
like Evan talks a lot,
and he goes to speak

00:01:02.229 --> 00:01:04.905
in conferences about entrepreneurship,

00:01:04.905 --> 00:01:06.674
and he's now famous for that.

00:01:06.674 --> 00:01:07.757
He's a brand.

00:01:08.773 --> 00:01:12.891
And I think, you know,
what is your number one?

00:01:12.891 --> 00:01:15.234
What is your product?
What is your big idea?

00:01:15.234 --> 00:01:17.372
It's you.
What's your point of view?

00:01:17.372 --> 00:01:19.267
And then the next question to ask yourself

00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:20.629
is why are you different?

00:01:20.629 --> 00:01:22.282
How do you differentiate yourself?

00:01:22.282 --> 00:01:24.420
Because, again, it's
a crowded marketplace,

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:25.947
it's a noisy marketplace.

00:01:25.947 --> 00:01:27.396
You want to be different.

00:01:27.396 --> 00:01:30.444
And then I think once you determine that,

00:01:30.444 --> 00:01:32.094
then you've got to build your profile.

00:01:32.094 --> 00:01:33.289
The great news about social

00:01:33.289 --> 00:01:35.016
is the audience can come to you.

00:01:35.016 --> 00:01:39.157
So, you know, if you're
talking about women in business

00:01:39.157 --> 00:01:41.909
or you're talking about inclusion

00:01:41.909 --> 00:01:45.242
or you're talking about breaking through

00:01:46.969 --> 00:01:50.402
the glass ceiling, whatever
it is, or exporting

00:01:50.402 --> 00:01:53.707
in terms of what are the lessons learned,

00:01:53.707 --> 00:01:56.107
if you're a U.S. business
and you want to export

00:01:56.107 --> 00:01:58.494
to Europe and Asia, whatever it is,

00:01:58.494 --> 00:02:00.979
you will find something where ...

00:02:00.979 --> 00:02:03.372
The good news is there
will be thousands of people

00:02:03.372 --> 00:02:05.478
out there who will want
to hear your story again.

00:02:05.478 --> 00:02:07.950
And then when you do speak,
and I'll say it kind of

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:11.083
how do you amplify the
message, and we've talked

00:02:11.083 --> 00:02:14.721
about this before in
(mumbles) around social media.

00:02:14.721 --> 00:02:16.904
I think that's a brilliant
way to build a following,

00:02:16.904 --> 00:02:21.071
build a brand, build your fame,
and the audience will come.

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:25.725
And then, you know,
you get things like DMs

00:02:25.725 --> 00:02:28.689
where people pop up and say
we'd like you to come and speak

00:02:28.689 --> 00:02:31.781
at this conference, and
I think with these things

00:02:31.781 --> 00:02:33.436
you should kind of test-drive it.

00:02:33.436 --> 00:02:35.654
What I'd advise then is develop

00:02:35.654 --> 00:02:38.173
some really compelling stories.

00:02:38.173 --> 00:02:40.071
And again, when you do stand up there,

00:02:40.071 --> 00:02:41.880
you want to tell them most of all

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.839
the authentic, tell them
your story, and again,

00:02:44.839 --> 00:02:47.352
put yourself, in sort of what we said,

00:02:47.352 --> 00:02:49.292
put yourself in the shoes of the audience.

00:02:49.292 --> 00:02:51.185
And obviously, if it's a big
conference of 2,000 people

00:02:51.185 --> 00:02:55.227
or 200 people, or even 20 people,
you want to kind of think,

00:02:55.227 --> 00:02:58.114
how do I make this really
interesting for the audience?

00:02:58.114 --> 00:03:00.298
What is likely to be on their mind?

00:03:00.298 --> 00:03:03.965
And what parts of my story
are going to kind of jump out

00:03:03.965 --> 00:03:06.348
for them and make this memorable?

00:03:06.348 --> 00:03:08.245
Because again, the amazing thing is

00:03:08.245 --> 00:03:10.186
we probably all go to conferences.

00:03:10.186 --> 00:03:13.067
Evan, you must have gone to
thousands of conferences.

00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:15.247
The question you ask yourself ...

00:03:15.247 --> 00:03:18.297
How do you become memorable?

00:03:18.297 --> 00:03:21.141
How do people talk about your
story that you presented?

00:03:21.141 --> 00:03:24.117
And they share that with your friends

00:03:24.117 --> 00:03:26.704
and then you have a moment,
you have a following.

00:03:26.704 --> 00:03:28.639
And literally, I know
the case I quote about

00:03:28.639 --> 00:03:31.266
someone I went to see speak 10 years ago,

00:03:31.266 --> 00:03:33.099
and they blew me away.

00:03:34.108 --> 00:03:35.752
And that could be you.

00:03:35.752 --> 00:03:38.526
So I think, I do think
it starts at the basics

00:03:38.526 --> 00:03:40.635
about the kind of big idea, the product,

00:03:40.635 --> 00:03:44.755
the product is you, build
your social media following,

00:03:44.755 --> 00:03:48.388
have a strong point of
view, and you will find then

00:03:48.388 --> 00:03:49.746
that you will get booked on,

00:03:49.746 --> 00:03:53.416
and you can do stuff,
I'm not a big fan of it,

00:03:53.416 --> 00:03:57.259
and I think it's likely
to be disintermediated,

00:03:57.259 --> 00:04:01.926
(audio skips) speaker marketing agencies

00:04:01.926 --> 00:04:04.199
that kind of book speakers
and they take a commission

00:04:04.199 --> 00:04:05.644
and all that sort of stuff.

00:04:05.644 --> 00:04:07.993
And a lot of famous
people and celebrities,

00:04:07.993 --> 00:04:11.252
use through their agents, these
speaker marketing agencies,

00:04:11.252 --> 00:04:15.419
but I think the way the reality
of the social media world

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:20.283
has sort of changed that forever.

00:04:20.283 --> 00:04:22.794
But what you might want
to do, when you get a bit

00:04:22.794 --> 00:04:26.009
of a following, if you did
want to do something around

00:04:26.009 --> 00:04:30.092
engaging an agent and one of these sort of

00:04:30.092 --> 00:04:33.059
speaker marketing agencies,
there's plenty of them.

00:04:33.059 --> 00:04:35.075
If you go to Toronto,
there's probably a dozen

00:04:35.075 --> 00:04:37.380
speaker marketing agencies there,

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:39.244
in New York, they're all over the place.

00:04:39.244 --> 00:04:41.766
But again, they will
have the way they do it.

00:04:41.766 --> 00:04:44.526
Companies like us,
we're doing Sage Summit,

00:04:44.526 --> 00:04:47.611
we want to book a speaker,
we want him talking about

00:04:47.611 --> 00:04:51.985
maybe general entrepreneurship
or how to overcome adversity

00:04:51.985 --> 00:04:56.152
or exporting or how do you
get ready to grow and scale

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:00.478
your business from being
a startup to scaleup.

00:05:00.478 --> 00:05:04.101
Whatever the theme is,
they'll go down the playbook

00:05:04.101 --> 00:05:06.079
of who we got on the books.

00:05:06.079 --> 00:05:08.429
It's almost like an actor sort of agent.

00:05:08.429 --> 00:05:10.218
They've got, who's got
these sort of skills,

00:05:10.218 --> 00:05:11.906
who can address this,

00:05:11.906 --> 00:05:13.552
and then they'll book you in there.

00:05:13.552 --> 00:05:16.562
I think, because this is very personal,

00:05:16.562 --> 00:05:19.235
and the product is you, I
think you should take control

00:05:19.235 --> 00:05:23.442
of that, and I'm always,
my advice would always ...

00:05:23.442 --> 00:05:26.004
I'm very reticent ... for you to sort of,

00:05:26.004 --> 00:05:29.432
this is a bad word, but sort
of prostitute your brand

00:05:29.432 --> 00:05:32.355
out there with lots of different
people so they dilute you.

00:05:32.355 --> 00:05:35.159
I think you want to be true to yourself,

00:05:35.159 --> 00:05:38.623
be congruent, be authentic, and be you.

00:05:38.623 --> 00:05:40.194
And then people will come to love you.

00:05:40.194 --> 00:05:42.668
If you've got a compelling
idea, a big vision,

00:05:42.668 --> 00:05:45.101
and you tell it in a great story.

00:05:45.101 --> 00:05:47.374
Wow, you will make yourself memorable.

00:05:47.374 --> 00:05:49.191
- I think to Steven's point,

00:05:49.191 --> 00:05:51.046
the gatekeepers are disappearing.

00:05:51.046 --> 00:05:53.519
Those people who you
have to go through them

00:05:53.519 --> 00:05:55.827
to get any opportunities, is disappearing.

00:05:55.827 --> 00:05:58.220
Those speaker houses,
the people who you need

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:00.036
their approval to get anything done,

00:06:00.036 --> 00:06:03.541
it's going away, which
creates a lot of opportunity

00:06:03.541 --> 00:06:06.798
for a lot of people,
but you have to be good.

00:06:06.798 --> 00:06:08.741
And you get good by practicing.

00:06:08.741 --> 00:06:10.104
You've got to practice.

00:06:10.104 --> 00:06:12.497
If you want to be a speaker, I
would be on YouTube everyday,

00:06:12.497 --> 00:06:16.073
practicing your speech,
practicing answering questions.

00:06:16.073 --> 00:06:17.845
Practicing, practicing,
practicing, practicing.

00:06:17.845 --> 00:06:19.989
Your talent should be obvious.

00:06:19.989 --> 00:06:22.213
There's this great clip that we included

00:06:22.213 --> 00:06:24.243
on one of our videos
of Eminem, the rapper,

00:06:24.243 --> 00:06:26.482
prank calling a radio station.

00:06:26.482 --> 00:06:29.036
He said his name was
like, I'm John from Miami,

00:06:29.036 --> 00:06:32.681
something like that, and he
said, hey can I rap for you?

00:06:32.681 --> 00:06:36.155
And he did some wrap, and LL
Cool J was on the station,

00:06:36.155 --> 00:06:38.918
and DJs were on the
station, and they said man

00:06:38.918 --> 00:06:42.473
you're really good,
like you should consider

00:06:42.473 --> 00:06:45.769
doing this as a career, John from Miami.

00:06:45.769 --> 00:06:48.336
And they didn't know it was Eminem,

00:06:48.336 --> 00:06:51.253
and later on he said it was Eminem,

00:06:52.172 --> 00:06:54.437
and he pulled their leg, and the thing is

00:06:54.437 --> 00:06:56.379
like his talent was obvious.

00:06:56.379 --> 00:06:58.030
So is your talent obvious.

00:06:58.030 --> 00:06:59.799
Steven talked about he
went to go see a speaker

00:06:59.799 --> 00:07:02.353
10 years ago that like blew him away.

00:07:02.353 --> 00:07:04.461
Are you doing that to your audiences?

00:07:04.461 --> 00:07:06.932
If you make a YouTube video
and the people who watch it

00:07:06.932 --> 00:07:10.021
are blown away, they're going to share it.

00:07:10.021 --> 00:07:11.379
They're going to tell
their friends about it.

00:07:11.379 --> 00:07:12.777
You're going to get
booked on speaker gigs.

00:07:12.777 --> 00:07:15.664
And the more you do that,
the more content you create

00:07:15.664 --> 00:07:18.189
that blows people away,
the more your profile

00:07:18.189 --> 00:07:20.082
is going to rise.

00:07:20.082 --> 00:07:21.940
The challenge is for most people,

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:23.545
you're not quite good enough yet.

00:07:23.545 --> 00:07:24.707
You need to practice more.

00:07:24.707 --> 00:07:26.145
You've got to get to that point

00:07:26.145 --> 00:07:27.299
where you can blow people away.

00:07:27.299 --> 00:07:28.863
Since the market is so wide open,

00:07:28.863 --> 00:07:30.593
you've got people around the world now

00:07:30.593 --> 00:07:33.311
you're competing with that
you have to get really good.

00:07:33.311 --> 00:07:36.655
And so it's never been
easier, which is great,

00:07:36.655 --> 00:07:38.919
but you have to put in
the work to get there,

00:07:38.919 --> 00:07:41.228
and so I would be starting a
YouTube channel immediately.

00:07:41.228 --> 00:07:44.526
I would be posting daily
content, work work work.

00:07:44.526 --> 00:07:47.914
I was not a great speaker when I started,

00:07:47.914 --> 00:07:51.074
but I did 4,000 YouTube videos, right?

00:07:51.074 --> 00:07:55.510
You better get better after
doing something 4,000 times.

00:07:55.510 --> 00:07:56.343
Anything.

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:01.971
If you're practicing basketball,
if you take 4,000 shots,

00:08:01.971 --> 00:08:04.242
you better get better at taking that shot.

00:08:04.242 --> 00:08:05.622
It's hard not to.

00:08:05.622 --> 00:08:09.435
And so I think getting
out there and practicing

00:08:09.435 --> 00:08:12.074
so that when people see
you, they are blown away,

00:08:12.074 --> 00:08:13.717
is going to be your ticket in,

00:08:13.717 --> 00:08:15.537
much more than trying to go to an agency

00:08:15.537 --> 00:08:18.304
or these houses, because the
gatekeepers are going away.

00:08:18.304 --> 00:08:20.471
To Steven's point.
I agree.

